<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	Hardened Runtime entitlements for Native Widgets Demo. What the build
	pipeline actually does, exactly, when `[macos] signing-identity` (see
	`frust.toml`) is set:
	  * The Hardened Runtime is always enabled at codesign time (codesign's
	    `options runtime` flag), for every identity, ad-hoc included.
	  * A secure timestamp (codesign's `timestamp` flag) is added only when
	    the identity is Apple-issued ("Developer ID Application:",
	    "Apple Distribution:", "Apple Development:", "Mac Developer:", or
	    "3rd Party Mac Developer Application:") — a secure timestamp needs
	    Apple's timestamp service, so an ad-hoc/self-signed local identity
	    is never made to depend on the network to sign.
	  * This file is handed to codesign (its `entitlements` flag) only when
	    it exists at `macos/app.entitlements` in the project — deleting it
	    drops entitlements from the signature entirely, it does not fall
	    back to a default set.
	Every entitlement below is commented out: a fresh scaffold builds and runs
	unsigned/ad-hoc-signed locally, which needs none of these, and asking
	Apple's notarization service for one the app doesn't actually use is a
	real review flag — uncomment only what the app needs.
	NOTE: never write two consecutive hyphens inside these comments (spell
	codesign flags without their dashes, as above). XML forbids that sequence
	in a comment, and while `plutil` tolerates it, the strict parser codesign
	runs (AMFI) rejects the whole file, failing every signed build.
	-->

	<!--
	JIT / dynamically-generated executable code. `frust`'s wgpu render
	pipeline (frust-engine) compiles shaders through Metal, not JIT'd Rust, so this is not
	needed by the framework itself — only by an app embedding something that
	genuinely requires it.
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
	<true/>
	-->

	<!--
	Outbound network access (HTTP calls, a backend plugin). Needed the
	moment the app talks to the network at all.
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
	-->

	<!--
	Inbound network access (the app itself listens for connections). Rarely
	needed by a client app.
	<key>com.apple.security.network.server</key>
	<true/>
	-->

	<!--
	Disables the App Sandbox entirely. Sandboxed is the default (this key
	absent, or explicitly `<false/>`) and the right starting point; only
	uncomment this if the app needs filesystem/device access the sandbox
	genuinely cannot express through a scoped entitlement.
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<false/>
	-->
</dict>
</plist>